  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe core idea is to apply a hybrid optimization strategy combining gradient descent with simulated annealing to explore the non-convex solution space effectively. This approach balances local improvements with global exploration by iteratively adjusting the step function h(x) to reduce the maximum overlap integral while maintaining constraints. The method leverages formal gradients for targeted updates and random perturbations to escape local minima. The workflow is structured to handle boundary conditions explicitly and maintain feasibility throughout optimization.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nRepresentation: h is a numpy array of length n_points, with entries h[i] \u2208 [0, 1], and sum(h) = n_points / 2. The objective function is defined as:\n\nC5 = max_{k} (2.0 / n_points) * sum_{i=0}^{n_points-1} h[i] * (1 - h[(i + k) % n_points])\n\nWhere k \u2208 [0, 2*n_points - 2] represents lags. For each k, compute the overlap integral using the discrete convolution equivalent: (2.0 / n_points) * Correlation(h, 1 - h, mode=\"full\")[k].\n\nConstrained optimization: \n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i \n- sum(h) = n_points / 2\n\nGradient calculation for lag k:\ngrad[i] = (2.0 / n_points) * [ (1 - h[(i + k) % n_points]) if (i + k) % n_points is valid, -h[(i - k) % n_points] if (i - k) % n_points is valid ]\n\nThe solver must ensure that all h[i] values and their sum remain feasible after each update.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nInputs: initial_h_values (if available), n_points, seed, budget_s\n\nStep 1: Parent validation\n- If initial_h_values is provided, copy to h and scale to [0,1] if needed\n- Ensure sum(h) \u2248 n_points/2 with absolute tolerance 1e-6\n- Clip out-of-bound values and normalize sum to exact value\n\nStep 2: Initialization\n- Set n_points = 512 (if not already provided)\n- If initial_h_values is available, upscale it to 512 samples via linear interpolation\n- Convert to numpy array h of length 512\n\nStep 3: Precompute essentials\n- dx = 2.0 / 512\n- lags = np.arange(0, 2*512 - 1)\n- max_overlap = np.inf\n- best_h = h.copy()\n\nStep 4: Iterative optimization\nFor iteration in 0 to 1000:\n   a. Compute overlaps for all lags using np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")\n   b. Identify k_max with max overlap value\n   c. Compute gradient vector for lag k_max\n   d. Update h = h - learning_rate * gradient\n   e. Clip h to [0,1] and normalize sum to 512/2\n   f. Perturb 5% of entries with \u00b10.1 with 50% probability\n   g. Re-normalize after perturbation\n   h. Recompute overlaps for k_max\n   i. If improvement > 1e-5, update best_h and reset learning rate\n   j. If stagnation > 300 iterations, reduce learning rate by 0.95\n\nStep 5: Final validation\n- Recompute overlaps for all lags\n- c5_bound = max_overlap * dx\n- Ensure 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and sum(h) = 512/2\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault configuration:\n- learning_rate = 0.05\n- temperature = 1.0 (for simulated annealing)\n- perturbation_rate = 0.05\n- iteration_limit = 1000\n- min_improvement = 1e-5\n- decay_factor = 0.95\n\nVariations:\n- Use multistart (5 random initializations if parent not available)\n- Switch to stochastic gradient descent after 500 iterations\n- Increase perturbation rate to 0.1 when improvement stalls\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nDominant costs:\n- 1000 iterations * (O(n) gradient + O(n) clipping) = ~2000 * 512 = 1e6 operations\n- CPU time estimate: ~150 seconds for 512 resolution\n- 500 seconds remaining for multistart or refinement\n\nRobustness handling:\n- If sum(h) deviates, apply:\n   h = (h - np.min(h)) / (np.max(h) - np.min(h)) \n   h = h / np.sum(h) * (512 / 2)\n- On solver failure, revert to initial_h_values resampled to 512\n- If any h[i] < 0 or > 1, clip and normalize\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFeasibility checks:\n- All h[i] \u2208 [0, 1] with absolute tolerance 1e-6\n- np.isclose(np.sum(h), 512/2, atol=1e-6)\n\nObjective consistency:\n- Verify c5_bound equals np.max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")) * dx\n\nOutput contract:\n- Return (h_values, c5_bound, n_points) as numpy array, float, int\n- h_values must be exactly length 512\n- c5_bound computed via evaluation metric\n- n_points = 512\n\nSanity checks:\n- Confirm index wrapping (i + k) % n_points handles all lags\n- Validate that dx = 2.0 / 512\n- Ensure perturbations don't violate constraints",
